//! member: true
//!
//! std.x.json — JSON as a QUERY, over the s40 kernel builtins: is this
//! text valid, what is at this path, what kind is it, how many children
//! does it have, and give it to me as the wolf type I expect.
//! Stability tier: **x (nursery, D31)**.
//!
//! ## The nursery banner (D31) — read this before you depend on it
//!
//! **`std.x.*` is not `std.*`.** A module under `x` is a RESIDENT rather
//! than a facade member — its path, its names, its rows and its behaviour
//! may change or disappear in a single sprint, and no other std module may
//! depend on one. `std/x/README.md` is the register: every resident is
//! listed there with the measured reason it is not in the facade and the
//! named trigger that graduates it.
//!
//! **The graduation clock.** At every campaign closeout each resident is
//! reviewed and one of three things happens, recorded in that closeout:
//! it GRADUATES (it moves under `std.`, and because the path *is* the
//! API, the move is the release note), it is DELETED (the reviewed
//! contract in its header is what survives), or it is EXTENDED by one
//! campaign with a written reason. Nothing sits in `x` unexamined.
//!
//! **This module's clock** starts at the stdc02 closeout, which is this
//! campaign's. Its named triggers are below.
//!
//! ## Why the first tenant is json
//!
//! D31 said the nursery's first tenant would be a json module, and s40
//! built the kernel for it — but the reason it is a resident rather than
//! a member is measured, not decreed, and it is two reasons:
//!
//! 1. **One executing lane.** `json_valid`/`json_get`/`json_type`/
//!    `json_len` are the checked tier's alone at s40: the native rung
//!    refuses them by name ("json builtins in native lowering (checked
//!    lane only at s40)") and lupin 0.1.6 has never heard of them. A core
//!    module with one lane out of three cannot make the promises the
//!    facade makes. The graduation trigger is the native mirror of
//!    `wolf_mem::json` plus the interpreter's own — the same two-consumer
//!    shape every other builtin tier went through.
//! 2. **This is a query surface, not the API json will end up with.** A
//!    dotted path is a small language and it cannot express what the
//!    document can hold: a key containing `.` is unreachable at this tier
//!    (documented upstream and here), there is no way to enumerate an
//!    object's keys, and every read re-parses the whole text. The API a
//!    program eventually wants is `std.json`'s DOM — one parse, a value
//!    you navigate — and that module already exists with everything
//!    except the parser (see the sibling note below). When it can parse,
//!    this module either graduates as the *streaming query* face beside
//!    it, or it is deleted and its callers move. That is a decision for a
//!    closeout, and this is where it is written down.
//!
//! ## `std.x.json` and `std.json` are two modules and one program may not
//! ## import both
//!
//! Module identity is the LAST path segment (F-0034), so `std.x.json` and
//! `std.json` are both `json` to an importer: `use std.json` beside
//! `use std.x.json` is `E0306` on both compiler rungs and `unsupported`
//! under lupin, measured at these pins (F-0058). Importing EITHER on its
//! own is fine, which is why this module can keep the name upstream gave
//! it. Every test and every doc example here imports exactly one.
//!
//! They divide the work the way their lanes do. `std.json` owns the DOM —
//! a `Value` enum you build, NAVIGATE and serialize. Navigation arrived
//! there at sc13: F-0037 (an enum returned through an error row takes the
//! miss path on every call) closed at lupin 0.1.10 and `json.get`/`at`
//! ship, so the sentence this paragraph carried for three sprints —
//! "the DOM cannot be walked" — is retired. What `std.json` still cannot
//! do is PARSE, and that is now a debt with a date rather than a
//! refusal: nothing blocks `parse` at these pins and §14 gives it one
//! sprint of grace. This module owns the read direction — text in, wolf
//! values out — and cannot build anything, because the kernel is queries
//! only.
//!
//! **Which means the graduation question this module's clock asks gets
//! sharper rather than easier.** The register says `x.json` either
//! graduates as the streaming QUERY face beside `std.json`'s DOM or is
//! deleted in favour of it; the DOM answering `parse` is exactly the
//! event that decides it, and it is one sprint away. Nothing about the
//! banner, the clock or the trigger changes here — this is a note for
//! whoever holds the stdc02 closeout, written where they will be
//! standing.
//!
//! ## The tier this delegates to
//!
//! **Capability-free.** The `json_*` builtins are the one pure builtin
//! family: no I13 capability, no sandbox category, nothing reached. A
//! package that uses only this module and the language declares no
//! capability at all. The comptime engine still refuses them — there is
//! no json evaluator in the D33 allowlist at v0, which is a design
//! decision and not a capability question.
//!
//! **And that refusal is the one thing in this repository that cannot be
//! held as a test.** `tests/{net,time,env}/comptime_refuses.lu` each pin
//! their tier's D33 rejection as `fail(E0701)` with the sandbox table's
//! own sentence ("reaches the clock, which comptime code can never
//! touch"). A `comptime fn` calling `json_valid` answers a bare
//! `unsupported` at resolve — no code, no reason string, nothing in the
//! record's `diagnostics` — so there is no directive to write it against
//! and a package author learns nothing about why. Filed as F-0060; there
//! is deliberately no `comptime_refuses.lu` here rather than a file
//! asserting something weaker than the truth.
//!
//! **Paths are dotted segments.** `""` is the root; a segment of digits
//! indexes an array; anything else keys an object. `"users.0.name"` is
//! the second element's `name`. Build one with `child`/`child_index`
//! rather than by hand. **A key containing `.` is unreachable** — the
//! tier says so and so does this module; it is not a bug to work around
//! here, it is the ceiling of a dotted path, and it is one of the reasons
//! the DOM is where json is going.
//!
//! **The rows are the toolchain's, verbatim** (§14's rule, and this
//! module adds no translation layer): `parse` for any RFC 8259 violation
//! in the DOCUMENT, `missing` for a path that addresses nothing, `kind`
//! for a node of the wrong kind for the operation. Two tags are this
//! module's own and they are marked as such below: `kind` is reused for
//! "the node is a number and you asked for an integer", and `overflow`
//! (§10's mark) for a number outside the wolf type you asked for.
//! Nothing here traps — a document is data, and every failure a document
//! can cause is a row.
//!
//! **Numbers keep their source spelling in the kernel** and this module
//! is where they become wolf values: `int_at` reads an integer exactly,
//! so a 64-bit id survives — `std.json`'s number posture, arrived at
//! from the other direction. The `f64` half is a contract below and the
//! reason is measured, not principled.
//!
//! **Every read re-parses the document.** `get` on a ten-megabyte text is
//! a ten-megabyte parse, and ten reads are ten parses. That is the
//! kernel's shape, not a defect this module can hide, and it is the
//! second-loudest argument for the DOM. Read a subtree once with `get`
//! (which hands back a valid JSON text) and query THAT, if the shape of
//! your data allows it.
//!
//! Reviewed contracts, blocked at the tier below:
//!
//! ```text
//! float_at(doc: str, path: str) -> f64 ! {parse, missing, kind, overflow}
//!     The number at `path` as `f64`, widening an integer node, with
//!     `std.fmt.decimal.parse_float`'s correct rounding. WRITTEN, and
//!     withdrawn before it shipped, for a reason that is arithmetic
//!     rather than design: `parse_float` is `unsupported` on BOTH
//!     compiler rungs ("arithmetic outside integers" at the mem tier —
//!     F-0026's f64 ceiling, one body deep), and the checked tier is
//!     this module's ONLY executing lane. So the function would have
//!     had zero lanes and zero evidence: no test could run it, no doc
//!     example could be fenced, and §14's doc-truth floor ("at least
//!     one lane reaches exit(0)") would have been missed on every one.
//!     A std function nobody can run is a claim, not code. It lands the
//!     day either compiler rung executes `parse_float`, with this
//!     signature and a four-line body; `get` plus the caller's own
//!     conversion is the spelling until then.
//! keys(doc: str, path: str) -> List[str] ! {parse, missing, kind}
//!     An object's member names. No builtin enumerates them — `json_len`
//!     counts members and nothing names them — so an object can be
//!     counted and not walked, which is the sharpest hole in the query
//!     tier (F-0058). Blocked, not unwritten.
//! items(doc: str, path: str) -> List[str] ! {parse, missing, kind}
//!     An array's elements as sub-documents. Writable TODAY over
//!     `json_len` plus `child_index` plus `get` in a loop, and left out
//!     deliberately: it would parse the document once per element, which
//!     is quadratic in a shape programs reach for constantly. It lands
//!     when the tier grows a cursor, or in the DOM.
//! parse(doc: str) -> Value   ·   stringify(v: Value) -> str
//!     The DOM directions, both `std.json`'s. `stringify` shipped at
//!     sc05 and `get`/`at` at sc13; `parse` is unblocked at these pins
//!     (F-0037 closed) and owed there next sprint, not here.
//! ```

use std.fmt

/// Whether `doc` is one well-formed RFC 8259 text.
///
/// Total: no rows, no traps — the one function here that answers for
/// every input, which is what makes it the guard the others are written
/// behind. Strict: no comments, no trailing commas, no bare identifiers,
/// exactly one value with only insignificant whitespace around it, and
/// container nesting limited (the kernel's limit is 128, and exceeding it
/// is invalidity rather than a stack fault).
///
/// Capability-free; refused at comptime (D33 — no json evaluator, not a
/// capability). Delegates to `json_valid`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// json.is_valid("[1, 2, 3]") == true
/// json.is_valid("{{\"a\": 1}}") == true
/// json.is_valid("[1,]") == false
/// json.is_valid("{{") == false
/// json.is_valid("") == false
/// ```
pub fn is_valid(doc: str) -> bool {
    json_valid(doc)
}

/// The node at `path`, rendered as text.
///
/// A string node comes back DECODED (escapes resolved, so `"a\nb"` is two
/// lines and not four characters); a number keeps its source spelling
/// exactly, so nothing is rounded on the way out; `true`, `false` and
/// `null` spell themselves; an object or array comes back as JSON text —
/// **a valid document in its own right**, which is what makes
/// `get(get(doc, "a"), "b")` work and is the cheapest way to query a
/// subtree twice.
///
/// Rows: `parse` when `doc` is not valid JSON, `missing` when nothing is
/// at `path`. Never traps.
///
/// Bare `get` is the checked-access spelling and this one can miss (§1).
///
/// Capability-free; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `json_get`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (json.get("{{\"a\": 1}}", "a") else "?") == "1"
/// (json.get("[10, 20]", "1") else "?") == "20"
/// (json.get("{{\"s\": \"hi\"}}", "s") else "?") == "hi"
/// (json.get("{{\"a\": {{\"b\": 2}}}}", "a.b") else "?") == "2"
/// (json.get("{{\"a\": 1}}", "b") else "?") == "?"
/// (json.get("{{", "a") else "?") == "?"
/// ```
pub fn get(doc: str, path: str) -> str ! {parse, missing} {
    json_get(doc, path)?
}

/// The kind of the node at `path` — one of `"object"`, `"array"`,
/// `"str"`, `"num"`, `"bool"`, `"null"`.
///
/// Rows: `parse`, `missing`. Never traps.
///
/// Spelled `type_name` and not `kind` for the reason `std.json` learned
/// the hard way: a function and a row tag with the same name in one
/// module make the tag resolve to the FUNCTION at the raise site,
/// silently (F-0036). The names here are the kernel's own vocabulary
/// (`"str"` and `"num"`, not RFC 8259's "string" and "number"), adopted
/// verbatim per §14 rather than prettified into a second dialect.
///
/// Capability-free; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `json_type`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (json.type_name("{{\"a\": [1]}}", "a") else "?") == "array"
/// (json.type_name("{{\"a\": [1]}}", "a.0") else "?") == "num"
/// (json.type_name("{{\"a\": \"x\"}}", "a") else "?") == "str"
/// (json.type_name("{{\"a\": null}}", "a") else "?") == "null"
/// (json.type_name("{{\"a\": true}}", "a") else "?") == "bool"
/// (json.type_name("{{\"a\": {{}}}}", "a") else "?") == "object"
/// ```
pub fn type_name(doc: str, path: str) -> str ! {parse, missing} {
    json_type(doc, path)?
}

/// How many elements an array has, or members an object has, at `path`.
///
/// Rows: `parse`, `missing`, and `kind` for a scalar — a number has no
/// length, and answering 1 or 0 would be a guess about which question was
/// asked. Never traps.
///
/// Not a string length: `len` of a `"str"` node is `kind`, and the length
/// of the text it holds is `std.str.len` of what `get` returns (§1: one
/// concept, one home).
///
/// Capability-free; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `json_len`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (json.len("[1, 2, 3]", "") else -1) == 3
/// (json.len("{{\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2}}", "") else -1) == 2
/// (json.len("[[1, 2]]", "0") else -1) == 2
/// (json.len("[]", "") else -1) == 0
/// (json.len("[1]", "0") else -1) == -1
/// ```
pub fn len(doc: str, path: str) -> int ! {parse, missing, kind} {
    json_len(doc, path)?
}

/// Whether `doc` has a node at `path`.
///
/// Rows: `parse` when the document is not valid JSON — a question about a
/// path has no honest answer when the text is not a document, and `false`
/// would say "no such field" about a file that is not JSON at all.
/// Never traps.
///
/// `has` rather than `contains` for a keyed lookup (§8's vocabulary).
///
/// Capability-free; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `json_valid`
/// and `json_get`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (json.has("{{\"a\": 1}}", "a") else false) == true
/// (json.has("{{\"a\": 1}}", "b") else false) == false
/// (json.has("{{\"a\": null}}", "a") else false) == true
/// (json.has("[1, 2]", "1") else false) == true
/// (json.has("[1, 2]", "9") else false) == false
/// (json.has("nope", "a") else true) == true
/// ```
pub fn has(doc: str, path: str) -> bool ! {parse} {
    if !json_valid(doc) {
        return parse
    }
    let probe = json_get(doc, path) else |_| { return false }
    probe.len >= 0
}

/// The text of the string node at `path`, decoded.
///
/// Rows: `parse`, `missing`, and `kind` when the node is not a string.
/// No coercion: `str_at` of a number is a miss and not `"42"`, because a
/// reader that guesses across JSON's types is a reader that loses the
/// document's own information. `get` is the function that renders any
/// node as text.
///
/// Capability-free; refused at comptime (D33).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (json.str_at("{{\"a\": \"wolf\"}}", "a") else "?") == "wolf"
/// (json.str_at("{{\"a\": \"line\\nbreak\"}}", "a") else "?") == "line\nbreak"
/// (json.str_at("{{\"a\": 1}}", "a") else "?") == "?"
/// ```
pub fn str_at(doc: str, path: str) -> str ! {parse, missing, kind} {
    let node = json_type(doc, path)?
    if node != "str" {
        return kind
    }
    json_get(doc, path)?
}

/// The boolean at `path`.
///
/// Rows: `parse`, `missing`, `kind` for every non-boolean node. No
/// coercion: `0`, `""` and `null` are all `kind`.
///
/// Capability-free; refused at comptime (D33).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (json.bool_at("{{\"a\": true}}", "a") else false) == true
/// (json.bool_at("{{\"a\": false}}", "a") else true) == false
/// (json.bool_at("{{\"a\": 0}}", "a") else false) == false
/// ```
pub fn bool_at(doc: str, path: str) -> bool ! {parse, missing, kind} {
    let node = json_type(doc, path)?
    if node != "bool" {
        return kind
    }
    let text = json_get(doc, path)?
    text == "true"
}

/// Whether the node at `path` is JSON `null`.
///
/// Rows: `parse`, `missing`. A predicate rather than a coercion, because
/// "is this null" has a yes-or-no answer for every node that EXISTS —
/// while an absent node is a different question, and it is `missing`.
///
/// Capability-free; refused at comptime (D33).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (json.is_null_at("{{\"a\": null}}", "a") else false) == true
/// (json.is_null_at("{{\"a\": 0}}", "a") else true) == false
/// (json.is_null_at("{{\"a\": null}}", "b") else true) == true
/// ```
pub fn is_null_at(doc: str, path: str) -> bool ! {parse, missing} {
    let node = json_type(doc, path)?
    node == "null"
}

/// The integer at `path`, exactly.
///
/// Rows: `parse`, `missing`; `kind` when the node is not a number OR is a
/// number that is not an integer (`1.5`, `1e3` and `1.0` are all `kind` —
/// the document wrote a non-integer and this function will not round
/// one); `overflow` (§10's mark) when the integer is outside `int`.
///
/// **`overflow` is a row and not a trap, and that is the whole reason
/// this function does its own range check** rather than delegating
/// straight to `std.fmt.parse_int`, which traps on an out-of-range value
/// (correctly: to `fmt` the text is a caller's argument). Here the digits
/// came out of a DOCUMENT — data, which §10 says raises where a caller's
/// mistake traps — so a 20-digit id in someone's JSON is an outcome this
/// function reports, never a process this function ends.
///
/// Exactness is the point: an id beyond 2^53 survives `int_at` and would
/// not survive `float_at`. That is the same number posture `std.json`
/// takes from the writing side.
///
/// Capability-free; refused at comptime (D33).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (json.int_at("{{\"a\": 42}}", "a") else -1) == 42
/// (json.int_at("{{\"a\": -7}}", "a") else 0) == -7
/// (json.int_at("{{\"a\": 9007199254740993}}", "a") else 0) == 9007199254740993
/// (json.int_at("{{\"a\": 1.5}}", "a") else -1) == -1
/// (json.int_at("{{\"a\": \"7\"}}", "a") else -1) == -1
/// (json.int_at("{{\"a\": 99999999999999999999}}", "a") else -1) == -1
/// ```
pub fn int_at(doc: str, path: str) -> int ! {parse, missing, kind, overflow} {
    let node = json_type(doc, path)?
    if node != "num" {
        return kind
    }
    let text = json_get(doc, path)?
    integer_of(text)?
}

/// `path` extended by one object key.
///
/// Total: no rows, no traps, and PURE — the only member of this module
/// that reaches no builtin, so it is the only one a comptime program
/// could use. An empty `path` is the root, so `child("", "a")` is `"a"`.
///
/// A `segment` containing `.` produces a path that addresses something
/// else, and this function does not pretend otherwise: there is no
/// escape in the dotted-path language to hide behind (the module header's
/// ceiling). Keys with dots need the DOM.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// json.child("", "users") == "users"
/// json.child("users", "0") == "users.0"
/// json.child("a.b", "c") == "a.b.c"
/// ```
pub fn child(path: str, segment: str) -> str {
    if path == "" {
        return segment
    }
    "{path}.{segment}"
}

/// `path` extended by one array index.
///
/// Total: no rows, no traps, and PURE — comptime-safe. A negative index
/// is not rejected here: it produces a path that addresses nothing, which
/// is the `missing` row at the query, and one refusal in the right place
/// beats two in the wrong one.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// json.child_index("", 0) == "0"
/// json.child_index("users", 2) == "users.2"
/// json.child_index("a.0", 1) == "a.0.1"
/// ```
pub fn child_index(path: str, i: int) -> str {
    child(path, "{i}")
}

/// The integer a JSON number's source text denotes. Private.
///
/// `kind` when the text is not an integer spelling at all (a fraction, an
/// exponent, an empty digit run); `overflow` when it is one and does not
/// fit `int`.
///
/// The range check is arithmetic rather than a comparison against a
/// literal, because the one value that cannot be built positively is the
/// one this has to admit: `int_min` is `-(922337203685477580 * 10) - 8`,
/// so the negative branch never forms the positive magnitude at all.
fn integer_of(text: str) -> int ! {kind, overflow} {
    var body = text
    var negative = false
    if body.starts_with("-") {
        negative = true
        body = body[1..]
    }
    if !is_digit_run(body) {
        return kind
    }
    let width = body.len
    if width > 19 {
        return overflow
    }
    if width < 19 {
        let small = fmt.parse_int(body) else |_| { return kind }
        if negative {
            return -small
        }
        return small
    }
    let head_text = body[0..18]
    let tail_text = body[18..19]
    let head = fmt.parse_int(head_text) else |_| { return kind }
    let tail = fmt.parse_int(tail_text) else |_| { return kind }
    let head_limit: int = 922337203685477580
    var tail_limit: int = 7
    if negative {
        tail_limit = 8
    }
    if head > head_limit {
        return overflow
    }
    if head == head_limit {
        if tail > tail_limit {
            return overflow
        }
    }
    if negative {
        return -head * 10 - tail
    }
    head * 10 + tail
}

/// Whether `s` is one or more ASCII digits and nothing else. Private.
///
/// Written with the recoverable slice rather than the language's, even
/// though the kernel has already promised this text is ASCII: a
/// non-boundary here would be the kernel breaking its promise, and a
/// `bounds` trap is not how a library should report that (§9's rule about
/// whose offset it is, taken the conservative way at a tier boundary).
fn is_digit_run(s: str) -> bool {
    let n = s.len
    if n == 0 {
        return false
    }
    var i: int = 0
    while i < n {
        let c = s.get(i..i + 1) else ""
        if !is_digit(c) {
            return false
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    true
}

/// Whether `c` is one ASCII digit. Private.
fn is_digit(c: str) -> bool {
    if c == "" {
        return false
    }
    let alphabet = "0123456789"
    alphabet.contains(c)
}
